schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: One job per event (educational / social / working / celebration)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A design constraint for community events: give each gathering exactly one
    of four jobs — educational (teach one thing), social (build relationships,
    no agenda), working (make concrete progress together), or celebration
    (mark a win, thank contributors) — rather than trying to do all of them in
    one evening.
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Structures the article's five-phase event-planning process; "define the
    job" is phase one, and the article argues social and working events are
    the most underrated for a young node (education attracts strangers,
    relationships and shared output make them stay).
  adjacent_meanings:
    - Priya Parker–style intentional gathering design (purpose-first event framing, referenced by name in a sibling article on gathering patterns rather than this one)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      "The single biggest mistake is asking one event to do everything" — the
      article explicitly contrasts this against combining teaching,
      relationship-deepening, decision-making, and celebration in one event.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    None flagged — a general event-facilitation heuristic, not attributed to a
    specific tradition or community.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/planning-web3-events.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
